# Where a workspace is kept

A workspace is the set of looms that one `.loom/config.yaml` file governs, and Loom assumes it is a folder. The compiler walks that folder to find the looms. The configuration service climbs out of it to find the configuration file. The language server reads files directly.

This chapter gives Loom two storage environments. The file system keeps working as it does today. Remote storage is any service that honours Loom's storage contract at a web address, or URL, and it keeps its data however it likes, in a database, an object store, or a vault. The looms stay the same plain text, the passes stay the same, and a new service, `WorkspaceSource`, hides the difference from the compiler and the language server.

## A loom stays text

A loom is text wherever it is kept. Every position in Loom counts characters into that text, so a diagnostic, a source mapping, and a rename edit all point at offsets in it. Remote storage may hold a loom in any shape, as one record or as many blocks. It must hand Loom the complete text, and the same bytes on every request until the loom changes.

The parsed tree stays derived. The `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` service builds it from the text, and the `LoomDiskCache` service keeps a copy only to save time, so deleting the cache loses nothing. A storage service that wants a tree of sections for its own queries derives one the same way.

## What a source must answer

The `WorkspaceSource` service is how Loom reaches a workspace. It names each loom by a location, a string that identifies the loom and means nothing to the compiler. It answers two questions that may take time, and four that may not.

The first two fetch text. The `open` method hands over a whole `Workspace` value, which holds the settings and the text of every loom. The `read` method hands over one loom's text again after that loom changes. Both may wait on a network, and both fail with the existing `ReadError`.

The other four answer at once. The `workspaceOf` method names the workspace a loom belongs to, and for a loom outside any workspace it names the loom's own folder. The `placeOf` method says where a sink of one of a workspace's looms lands, from the `Workspace` value that the `open` method handed over. The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods cross between a location and a uniform resource identifier, or URI, the address that the Language Server Protocol uses.

A `Location` is a string. In this chapter's first step it is the compiler's own `Path` type under a second name, and the `Path` type gives way to it in the fourth.

=>

export type Location = Path

export interface Workspace {
  readonly location: Location
  readonly manifest: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest>
  readonly looms: ReadonlyMap<Location, string>
}

export interface WorkspaceSourceApi {
  readonly open: (workspace: Location) => Effect.Effect<Workspace, ReadError>
  readonly read: (loom: Location) => Effect.Effect<string, ReadError>
  readonly workspaceOf: (loom: Location) => Location
  readonly placeOf: (workspace: Workspace, loom: Location, sink: string) => string
  readonly locationOf: (uri: URI) => Location
  readonly uriOf: (loom: Location) => URI
}

~

`WorkspaceSource` takes the shape of the `FrameQuery` and `Composition` capabilities in Part VII's chapter "The service contract". Its own layer dies, because only an environment's layer can say where looms are kept.

=>

export class WorkspaceSource extends Context.Service<WorkspaceSource>()('WorkspaceSource', {
  make: Effect.die('WorkspaceSource must be provided by an environment') as Effect.Effect<WorkspaceSourceApi>,
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

~

Loom ships one source for each environment. The `FileSystemSource` layer reads folders the way Loom does today. The `RemoteSource` layer speaks the storage contract. Both provide the `WorkspaceSource` service, and a test can supply its own through the `Layer.succeed` function.

## Folders as a source

The `FileSystemSource` layer keeps a workspace in a folder on a disk. A location is an absolute path there, so every loom in a folder keeps the name it has today. A read is the synchronous file read that `DocumentSource` makes, the compiler's `fromDisk` function, so it never waits.

The `open` method takes the workspace's folder. It reads the folder's `.loom/config.yaml` file through the `LoomConfig` service, then walks the corpus folder the manifest names, `corpus` unless the manifest names another. The `loomsIn` function, which the chapter "The looms in a project" describes, does the walk under the project's rules. So `open` hands over the same looms that the compiler builds and the tangler writes. A folder that holds no configuration opens as a loose workspace, with no manifest and every loom under the folder.

=>

const corpusOf = (workspace: Location, manifest: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest>): Location =>
  Option.match(manifest, {
    onNone: () => workspace,
    onSome: (m) => join(workspace, m.corpus ?? defaultCorpus),
  })

~

The `placeOf` method resolves a sink the way the `outputPath` function resolves it. A sink lands under the package root, the folder above the nearest corpus folder, or beside its loom when the loom sits in no corpus folder. The `containerRoot` function of the chapter "A project's configuration" finds the package root from the location and the manifest that the `Workspace` value carries, so `placeOf` reads nothing from the disk.

=>

const packageRootOf = (workspace: Workspace, loom: Location): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.flatMap(workspace.manifest, (m) =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(containerRoot(dirname(loom), m.corpus ?? defaultCorpus, workspace.location)),
  )

~

The `locationOf` method takes a file URI's path, and keeps any other URI whole as its location. So `memory:/a.loom` never collapses into the path `/a.loom`, as the `fsPath` property would make it. The `uriOf` method turns an absolute path back into a file URI, and parses any other location as the URI it was.

=>

const locationOfUri = (uri: URI): Location => (uri.scheme === 'file' ? uri.fsPath : uri.toString())

const uriOfLocation = (loom: Location): URI => (isAbsolute(loom) ? URI.file(loom) : URI.parse(loom))

~

The layer gathers the six answers. The `workspaceOf` method climbs from the loom's folder through the `findWorkspace` function, the climb the configuration makes, and names the loom's own folder when no folder above it holds a configuration. The layer takes the `LoomConfig` and `PackageConfig` services from the program that provides it.

=>

export const FileSystemSource = Layer.effect(
  WorkspaceSource,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const config = yield* LoomConfig
    const packages = yield* PackageConfig
    const open = (workspace: Location): Effect.Effect<Workspace, ReadError> =>
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const manifest = Option.fromNullishOr(yield* config.manifest(workspace))
        const settings = yield* packages.resolve(workspace)
        const paths = yield* Effect.sync(() =>
          loomsIn(corpusOf(workspace, manifest), projectIn(settings)),
        )
        const looms = yield* Effect.forEach(paths, (loom) =>
          fromDisk(loom).pipe(Effect.map((text) => [loom, text] as const)),
        )
        return { location: workspace, manifest, looms: new Map(looms) }
      })
    const source: WorkspaceSourceApi = {
      open,
      read: fromDisk,
      workspaceOf: (loom) =>
        Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(findWorkspace(dirname(loom))), () => dirname(loom)),
      placeOf: (workspace, loom, sink) => outputPath(packageRootOf(workspace, loom), loom, sink),
      locationOf: locationOfUri,
      uriOf: uriOfLocation,
    }
    return source
  }),
)

~

## Why the text waits in memory

The language server cannot wait for a network. Volar calls the `createVirtualCode` hook synchronously, and the `LoomLanguagePlugin` module answers that call with the `runtime.runSync` method. It answers diagnostics, go-to-definition, and rename the same way. The `runSync` method fails on any effect that has to wait, so a network read cannot run inside those calls.

The text must therefore be in memory before the editor asks for it. The server calls the `open` method while it handles the `initialize` request, which Volar lets it await, and the `DocumentSource` service holds what comes back. Its `hold` method keeps every loom's text, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes. The compiler keeps reading through the `DocumentSource` service as it does today, and each read of a held loom becomes a lookup in memory.

In short, the `WorkspaceSource` service fetches and the `DocumentSource` service remembers.

Holding the text costs almost nothing, because Loom already holds it. Each `LoomModule` value carries its loom's text, and the `warmCorpus` function builds the whole corpus when the server starts. The `tangle` verb reads the whole corpus too, on every run.

An editor can also open a loom from a workspace the server has not opened yet. The file-system source handles this during a synchronous call, because its reads never wait. The remote source never meets the case, because its URL names one workspace.

## The storage contract

Remote storage answers two requests, because only the `open` and `read` methods fetch anything. The other four methods must answer at once, so they cannot cross a network, and the `RemoteSource` layer answers them by rule.

A URL names one workspace, and each loom's location is a URL beneath it, ending in `.loom`. Volar's language lookup and the `isLoom` function both read that ending. Every request carries the secret key in an `Authorization: Bearer` header.

A `GET` request on the workspace's URL returns the workspace as JSON. It holds the contract's version, the workspace's manifest, and every loom's text keyed by location.

```
{
  "contract": 1,
  "manifest": { "corpus": "corpus", "primary": "TypeScript" },
  "looms": {
    "https://storage.example/books/42/corpus/book.loom": "---\nPart: ..."
  }
}
```

A `GET` request on a loom's location returns that loom's text.

An Effect Schema defines the JSON shape, and a service written in any language can honour it. New versions of the contract only add fields, so an older Loom still reads a newer service.

The contract holds no write request. The server never saves a loom, so writing belongs to whoever edits the text. Permissions, conflicts, and history belong there too.

The `RemoteSource` layer answers the other four methods from the URL. The `workspaceOf` method always names that URL. The `placeOf` method returns the sink unchanged, so a sink stays relative to the workspace. The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods pass a URI through as a string and parse it back.

## The URL and the key

Two environment variables connect Loom to remote storage. The `LOOM_STORAGE_URL` variable names the workspace, and the `LOOM_STORAGE_KEY` variable holds the secret key. When the first is set, Loom uses the `RemoteSource` layer, and the second must be set too. When the first is unset, Loom uses the `FileSystemSource` layer.

Neither value belongs in the `.loom/config.yaml` file. On the file system that file sits in a repository. In remote storage, Loom needs the key to fetch that file in the first place.

The key stays in an Effect `Redacted` value, so a log line cannot print it. A wrong key fails the `open` method, so the editor hears of it as it connects.

The two variables are all the language server needs, so it can run on an author's machine or next to the storage.

## A location is a name, not a path

The compiler compares locations and keys its maps by them, and it never takes one apart. Only the source knows what a location means, because the source makes it. The `Path` type becomes `Location` to say so.

The file-system source keeps today's absolute paths as locations, so nothing changes for a loom in a folder.

The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods replace every use of `uri.fsPath`, `URI.file`, and `fileURLToPath` in the `LoomLanguagePlugin`, `LoomServer`, and `LoomLanguage` modules. This removes a trap as well. The `fsPath` property ignores a URI's scheme, so `memory:/a.loom` and `file:///a.loom` both come out as `/a.loom`.

One form of anchor still reads inside a location. The anchor `::[Name](path.loom)` goes through the `resolveRelative` function, which splits the location on `/`. A URL splits the same way, so this anchor keeps working until the chapter "Names through the book" retires it.

## Four folder rules move out

Four rules about folders sit in the compiler and its neighbours. Each one moves into the file-system source or behind a layer, so the compiler no longer depends on a folder.

**Which looms belong.** The `listFrom` function finds the corpus folder, then uses the `holds` and `loomsIn` functions to walk it under the rules in `.gitignore` files. The chapter "The looms in a project" describes that walk, and none of it means anything without folders. The walk becomes part of the file-system source's `open` method, and the compiler takes the keys of the `Workspace.looms` map instead. The `list` method of the `Source` interface loses its folder and project arguments.

**Where a workspace begins.** The `LoomConfig` service climbs folders to find the `.loom/config.yaml` file, then works out `packageRoot` and `corpusDir` from folder names. The `PackageConfig` service now reads the manifest the workspace holds. The `BuildSettings` interface keeps what a manifest says, such as the anchor delimiters, the primary language, the variables, and the width.

**Where a sink lands.** The `outputPath` function resolves a sink against the parent of the corpus folder, or against the loom's own folder when the loom sits outside any corpus folder. So a loom's folder can decide where its output goes. The `outputPath` function moves into the file-system source as its `placeOf` method. The compiler stores the answer in each `TangledFile` and `ComposedFile` value and never reads it.

**What the cache may keep.** The `LoomDiskCache` service writes to the `.loom/cache` folder, and it reads the loom's file to check that it is not keeping an unsaved edit. That check now compares against the text the `DocumentSource` service holds. The `LoomCompiler` layer provides this cache itself today, and the entry point provides it instead. Remote storage can then run with a cache that keeps nothing, and the server rebuilds the corpus at each start.

## How the server hears of a change

The server needs no new signal. Editors already report changes through the protocol's `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles` notification, and the `LoomServer` module already reacts to it. The notification carries a URI of any scheme, so an editor working over remote storage sends the same message.

The handler changes in one way. It calls the `read` method for each changed loom, updates the `DocumentSource` service, and then invalidates as it does today. That read may wait, so this work moves out of the `runSync` call and into the fiber the handler already forks to reproject open looms.

The server recognises a change to the settings by the ending of its URI, `/.loom/config.yaml`, and then calls the `open` method again.

The server never saves a loom. Rename and formatting return edits, and the editor applies them and saves. An editor working over remote storage saves through its own service, then sends the notification.

## What keeps its folder

Three things stay on disk.

**What the command line writes.** The `tangle` and `weave` commands only read looms, so they read from either environment and write their output to a folder. With the two variables set, the `loom tangle` command fetches a workspace from remote storage and writes its product to the current folder. This works because the `tangle` verb returns `TangledFile` values and writes nothing. The lock stays beside that output, because it records what the tangle wrote. The `format`, `toc`, and `set` commands rewrite a workspace's own text, so they work only on the file system.

**The programs Loom runs.** The `.loom/services` folder and the `~/.loom/typescript` folder hold language services and TypeScript 7. Neither holds a workspace's content, so both stay on the machine that runs the server. The `LOOM_HOME` variable already moves the services out of the workspace folder.

**Language services that need a folder.** TypeScript 7 runs as `tsc --lsp` in a real folder and reads `tsconfig.json` and `node_modules` from it. A workspace with no folder gets every feature Loom answers itself: its own diagnostics, go-to-definition, find-references, rename, semantic tokens, composition, and weaving. It gets no type checking from TypeScript until something gives that service a folder. That limit belongs to the language service, not to the compiler.

## Choosing the environment at startup

The `LoomServer` module builds its `ManagedRuntime` value as a module constant, over the `DocumentSource.layer` value. That constant becomes a value the `startLanguageServer` function builds from a layer it receives. The server's entry point reads the two variables and passes the matching layer, and the `loom` program does the same. The rest of the service graph stays as it is.

## A workspace with no folder

Two tests decide when this chapter is done. Both run a small storage service on a local port, which holds its looms in a map behind a key. That service is also the stub a consumer of the contract starts from.

The first test starts the language server with that service's URL and key, and with no folder. It opens a loom and receives diagnostics, follows a definition into another loom, renames a section across looms, and reads the composed files. It then sends a change notification and watches the diagnostics move. Every read crosses a real socket and really waits, so any code still reading inside a synchronous call fails this test.

The second test starts the server with a wrong key and expects the failure during the `initialize` request, with no key in the message.

The existing tests keep passing, with their stubs updated to the new shapes. The `loom tangle` command writes the same bytes over this book.

## Testing the file-system source

The cases build a workspace in a temporary folder: a `.loom/config.yaml` file, a book at the root of the corpus folder, and a chapter in a part's folder beneath it. A loose folder beside the workspace holds one loom and no configuration.

Opening the workspace hands over its manifest and both looms with their text. A loom names its workspace, and the loose loom names its own folder. A sink of the book lands in the workspace's folder, which is the package root, and a sink of the loose loom lands beside it. A file URI crosses to its path and back, and a `memory:` URI keeps its scheme both ways.

`DocumentSource` answers a held loom from memory. The last case holds the opened workspace's looms and rewrites the book on the disk. A read of the book still returns the text that `DocumentSource` held, and a read of a loom it never held returns the text on the disk. Once `DocumentSource` forgets the book, a read of the book returns the rewritten text on the disk.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Effect, Layer, ManagedRuntime, Option } from 'effect'
import { mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, realpathSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { DocumentSource } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { PackageConfig } from '../src/PackageConfig'
import { FileSystemSource, WorkspaceSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'

const runtime = ManagedRuntime.make(
  Layer.mergeAll(FileSystemSource, DocumentSource.layer).pipe(
    Layer.provide(PackageConfig.layer),
    Layer.provide(LoomConfig.layer),
  ),
)

const source = () => runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.map((s) => s)))

const folder = (prefix: string): string => realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), prefix)))

const workspace = () => {
  const root = folder('loom-workspace-')
  mkdirSync(join(root, '.loom'))
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus', 'part'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), 'corpus: corpus\n')
  const book = join(root, 'corpus', 'book.loom')
  const chapter = join(root, 'corpus', 'part', 'chapter.loom')
  writeFileSync(book, '# Book\n')
  writeFileSync(chapter, '# Chapter\n')
  return { root, book, chapter }
}

const looseLoom = () => {
  const root = folder('loom-loose-')
  const loom = join(root, 'loose.loom')
  writeFileSync(loom, '# Loose\n')
  return { root, loom }
}

describe('FileSystemSource — a workspace kept in a folder', () => {
  it('opens a workspace with its manifest and the text of every loom', async () => {
    const { root, book, chapter } = workspace()
    const opened = await runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((s) => s.open(root))))
    expect(opened.location).toBe(root)
    expect(Option.isSome(opened.manifest)).toBe(true)
    expect([...opened.looms.keys()].sort()).toEqual([book, chapter].sort())
    expect(opened.looms.get(chapter)).toBe('# Chapter\n')
  })

  it("names a loom's workspace, or its own folder when it has none", async () => {
    const { root, chapter } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const s = await source()
    expect(s.workspaceOf(chapter)).toBe(root)
    expect(s.workspaceOf(loose.loom)).toBe(loose.root)
  })

  it('places a sink under the package root, or beside a loose loom', async () => {
    const { root, book } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const s = await source()
    const opened = await runtime.runPromise(s.open(root))
    const looseOpened = await runtime.runPromise(s.open(loose.root))
    expect(s.placeOf(opened, book, 'src/book.ts')).toBe(join(root, 'src', 'book.ts'))
    expect(s.placeOf(looseOpened, loose.loom, 'loose.ts')).toBe(join(loose.root, 'loose.ts'))
  })

  it('crosses between locations and URIs without losing a scheme', async () => {
    const { book } = workspace()
    const s = await source()
    expect(s.locationOf(URI.file(book))).toBe(book)
    expect(s.uriOf(book).toString()).toBe(URI.file(book).toString())
    expect(s.locationOf(URI.parse('memory:/a.loom'))).toBe('memory:/a.loom')
    expect(s.uriOf('memory:/a.loom').scheme).toBe('memory')
  })

  it('lets DocumentSource answer a held loom from memory and any other from disk', async () => {
    const { root, book } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const read = (path: string) =>
      runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.read(path))))
    const opened = await runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((s) => s.open(root))))
    await runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.hold(opened.looms))))
    writeFileSync(book, '# Rewritten\n')
    expect(await read(book)).toBe('# Book\n')
    expect(await read(loose.loom)).toBe('# Loose\n')
    await runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.forget(book))))
    expect(await read(book)).toBe('# Rewritten\n')
  })
})

~

## What the source draws on

Effect supplies the service, the layer, `Option`, and the effect type. Node's `path` takes a location apart, and joins a corpus folder to its workspace. The `vscode-uri` package parses and builds the URIs the editor speaks. From `@athrio/loom-config` come the `LoomConfig` service, the `WorkspaceManifest` type, and the `findWorkspace`, `containerRoot`, and `defaultCorpus` rules this source shares with the configuration. `Path` types a compiler path, and `ReadError` names a read that failed. The compiler lends `fromDisk`, which reads a file, and `outputPath`, which resolves a sink. `PackageConfig` gives the project's settings, and `loomsIn` and `projectIn` walk its corpus.

=>

import { Context, Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { dirname, isAbsolute, join } from 'node:path'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import {
  containerRoot,
  defaultCorpus,
  findWorkspace,
  LoomConfig,
  type WorkspaceManifest,
} from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { type Path } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { type ReadError } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { fromDisk, outputPath } from './LoomCompiler'
import { PackageConfig } from './PackageConfig'
import { loomsIn, projectIn } from './LoomProject'

~

